01:41.470 --> 01:44.838
Councillor McCabe. Present. Councillor Nowak. Present.

01:45.014 --> 01:48.416
Councillor Salonen. Present. Councillor Shantz. Present.

01:48.598 --> 01:51.080
Councillor Vrbanovic. Present. Perfect,

01:51.380 --> 01:54.000
Thank you. Councillor Williams. Present.

01:54.900 --> 01:59.068
And Councillor Wolf. Present. Thank you. Thank you.

01:59.204 --> 02:02.748
As we begin this meeting, we are again reminded that many of our homes

02:02.764 --> 02:06.012
and places of employment in Waterloo Region are

02:06.036 --> 02:09.372
situated on the land that is the traditional territory of

02:09.396 --> 02:13.516
Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabe, and Chonnonton peoples.

02:13.708 --> 02:17.004
We recognize and deeply appreciate their historic connection

02:17.052 --> 02:20.990
to Waterloo region, And we also recognize the contributions of

02:21.030 --> 02:24.190
Indigenous Peoples have made in shaping and strengthening this

02:24.230 --> 02:27.902
community. We are grateful for the opportunity to

02:27.926 --> 02:31.582
live and work here and reaffirm our collective commitment to

02:31.606 --> 02:34.958
keep the promise that reconciliation takes place through

02:35.014 --> 02:39.230
learning and community building with the Indigenous People who continue to

02:39.270 --> 02:43.550
live here. We are all treaty people with a responsibility to

02:43.590 --> 02:45.130
honor all of our relations.

02:48.110 --> 02:52.406
Are there any declarations of pecuniary interest under the Municipal Conflict

02:52.438 --> 02:55.530
of Interest Act? None.

02:56.350 --> 02:59.290
We have no presentations,

02:59.950 --> 03:03.750
we have no delegations and

03:03.790 --> 03:08.330
we have nothing on the consent agenda.

03:09.470 --> 03:13.446
Councillor McCabe. I just was curious about the timing for this because

03:13.518 --> 03:16.830
originally in the agenda. Oh, were you about to address that?

03:16.990 --> 03:20.462
My apologies, I was going to mention that at the beginning. So what we're going

03:20.486 --> 03:24.334
to do is work through until 12:00 and then depending

03:24.382 --> 03:27.342
where we are on the agenda, if that's okay with everybody, we'll take a half

03:27.366 --> 03:29.650
an hour break and then reconvene.

03:30.870 --> 03:34.090
Does that sound okay with everyone?

03:37.190 --> 03:40.942
So the first item, 8.1,

03:40.966 --> 03:44.558
the strategic priorities Homes for all. I'd like to call on Ryan Pettipierre,

03:44.574 --> 03:47.982
the Director of Housing Services, to introduce the report on

03:48.006 --> 03:51.162
Building Better Futures Update and next steps.

03:51.226 --> 03:54.650
You're on Ryan. Thank you, Chair Erb and

03:54.730 --> 03:58.522
Good morning Regional Council. It's good to have this report and

03:58.546 --> 04:02.202
presentation in front of you this morning. I've got about six slides

04:02.266 --> 04:04.890
that I will run you through, give you a bit of a context in terms

04:04.930 --> 04:08.010
of where we've been, where we find ourselves at the moment,

04:08.090 --> 04:12.070
and where we hope to take this work over the next five years.

04:12.770 --> 04:15.680
Go to the next slide again. Thank you.

04:16.300 --> 04:20.100
So, as no surprise to any of you, we've got a lot of

04:20.140 --> 04:23.348
important work to accomplish in housing and homelessness.

04:23.524 --> 04:27.492
Work that has both a broad scope and is very complex in

04:27.516 --> 04:31.588
nature. What helps guide us through all of that complexity

04:31.684 --> 04:35.252
and scope are our plans. So we've got plans,

04:35.316 --> 04:38.852
lots of plans. Plans that are intrinsically related and

04:38.876 --> 04:42.912
depend on one another for success in achieving homes for all in

04:42.936 --> 04:46.928
this region. You're well aware of our plan to end chronic homelessness,

04:47.024 --> 04:51.136
that is at its heart, looking to prevent experiences of homelessness whenever

04:51.168 --> 04:54.544
and wherever possible, and that when it does occur, it is rare,

04:54.592 --> 04:57.952
it is brief, and that it doesn't happen again. The plan I'll be

04:57.976 --> 05:01.968
speaking in more detail to you about this morning is our Building

05:02.024 --> 05:05.232
Better Futures framework that in its initial

05:05.296 --> 05:09.104
iteration was a vision of creating 25 new

05:09.192 --> 05:12.016
affordable homes in a period of five years.

05:12.168 --> 05:15.852
In the iteration that that I'll be sharing with you through this

05:15.876 --> 05:19.852
presentation and report, we're looking to revise that target to

05:19.876 --> 05:23.100
creating 3,500 affordable homes in that five

05:23.140 --> 05:26.220
year period with targets specifically to low and

05:26.260 --> 05:29.480
very low income households across this region.

05:29.940 --> 05:33.484
And related and nested within that Building Better Futures frame

05:33.532 --> 05:37.084
and vision is our Waterloo Region Housing Revitalization

05:37.212 --> 05:40.780
Plan. Brought an update to you in December of last

05:40.820 --> 05:44.552
year on this work. This is a plan specific to the housing stock that

05:44.576 --> 05:47.832
we own and operate and are looking to redevelop in the

05:47.856 --> 05:51.064
form of six of our sites to create at least 600

05:51.152 --> 05:54.580
net new units through that process.

05:55.040 --> 05:58.664
Next slide please. to show you how these

05:58.752 --> 06:02.856
plans are related and just having another look at them, I've shared

06:02.888 --> 06:05.512
this slide with you before, but I think it's helpful just again to go over

06:05.536 --> 06:09.200
the context, understand how these plans work together

06:09.320 --> 06:13.200
to accomplish the strategic vision of homes for all in this

06:13.240 --> 06:16.976
region. The top of this slide you'll see Housing continuum.

06:17.008 --> 06:20.672
So this should be familiar. It's a bit of a simplistic overlay

06:20.736 --> 06:24.592
of the housing system from homelessness

06:24.656 --> 06:28.128
on the left hand side all the way through various other forms of housing

06:28.144 --> 06:32.140
up until market home ownership on the far right hand side

06:32.680 --> 06:36.928
of the slide as well. I'll give the caveat that this

06:36.984 --> 06:40.492
does simplify a very complex system. We know

06:40.516 --> 06:44.364
in its ideal form we'll have enough supports and services along that entire continuum to

06:44.372 --> 06:47.692
Support people wherever they are and help move them from left to

06:47.716 --> 06:50.828
right. It doesn't always work like that.

06:50.964 --> 06:54.460
People don't continue to move. People can stop moving. People can move

06:54.500 --> 06:57.820
backwards and forwards. But I think for illustrative purposes,

06:57.900 --> 07:01.200
in describing our plans and how we target the work, I think it helps.

07:01.540 --> 07:04.780
You'll see underneath the continuum, there's a black bar that illustrates

07:04.860 --> 07:08.044
where the plan to End chronic homelessness sits in the areas of

07:08.052 --> 07:11.376
the housing continuum that that plan targets. Beside that, you'll see a green

07:11.408 --> 07:15.040
bar that illustrates where our Waterloo Regional Housing Plan sits

07:15.200 --> 07:19.040
and targets. And underneath that, overlapping both the Plan

07:19.080 --> 07:22.960
to End Chronic Homelessness and the Waterloo Regional Housing Revitalization

07:23.040 --> 07:26.464
Plan, we have the plan that I'm talking about to you in more detail today,

07:26.552 --> 07:29.856
that's Building Better Futures, that spans the continuum from supportive

07:29.888 --> 07:33.300
housing all the way through to affordable home ownership.

07:34.200 --> 07:37.648
So I think it's just good context to understand how these plans work

07:37.704 --> 07:41.518
together and support populations across

07:41.574 --> 07:44.750
the region in various different ways. Underneath those

07:44.790 --> 07:48.174
bars of the plan, we've got the places and spaces

07:48.222 --> 07:51.742
that constitute each form of housing that's laid out

07:51.926 --> 07:55.982
on the continuum in terms of the stock that we

07:56.006 --> 07:59.550
support in those various forms. And underneath that is

07:59.590 --> 08:03.198
a percentage of the budget that is allocated to each of those forms

08:03.294 --> 08:07.328
of housing. So I think this is just a good reminder and

08:07.384 --> 08:10.912
illustration not only of how we target our frameworks and

08:10.936 --> 08:14.912
our initiatives, but how we target the budget to support those

08:14.936 --> 08:18.224
initiatives Along the way. and you'll see that again,

08:18.312 --> 08:21.888
the lion's share of investment and effort is trying

08:22.024 --> 08:25.872
to solve homelessness overall in the forms of permanent

08:25.936 --> 08:29.376
housing. That work being done through Waterloo

08:29.408 --> 08:33.200
Regional Housing Revitalization Plan and our work in

08:33.240 --> 08:36.510
building better futures. Next slide.

08:36.850 --> 08:37.630
Thanks.

08:40.290 --> 08:43.754
So to jump back to Building Better Futures

08:43.882 --> 08:47.978
in its original iteration, this was implemented in

08:48.034 --> 08:51.146
2021 with really just,

08:51.218 --> 08:54.426
It was a vision. It was a vision of doing more in the affordable

08:54.458 --> 08:57.658
housing space than we had done historically. When we looked

08:57.674 --> 09:01.226
at the numbers of affordable housing that we were creating, producing, or supporting

09:01.258 --> 09:04.940
in this region historically, we were producing about 50 units in

09:04.980 --> 09:08.412
any given year. We wanted to not only do more than that,

09:08.436 --> 09:11.468
we wanted to do greatly more than that. So we started with a vision of

09:11.524 --> 09:14.520
doing tenfold more in this space.

09:14.980 --> 09:18.412
And how we proposed to do that was looking at a number

09:18.436 --> 09:22.604
of creative and innovative approaches through funding grants,

09:22.732 --> 09:26.092
leveraging our own Waterloo Regional Housing stock, use of

09:26.116 --> 09:29.644
surplus lands and increased use of portable rent

09:29.692 --> 09:33.516
supplements. We also had some significant funding supported by council

09:33.668 --> 09:36.840
to kickstart that effort to the tune of about $20 million

09:36.920 --> 09:39.460
In 2021 and 2022.

09:40.080 --> 09:43.272
And when we started this work, we didn't know if

09:43.296 --> 09:46.968
we were going to be successful or not. But we knew we wanted to try

09:47.104 --> 09:50.180
and we knew that this region needed us to try.

09:50.560 --> 09:54.328
And the success that we have had to date has

09:54.384 --> 09:58.504
been not only through those innovative approaches that we've

09:58.552 --> 10:02.224
taken, but also how we've organized ourselves internally.

10:02.352 --> 10:06.980
The way that we do this work is completely cross departmental

10:07.320 --> 10:11.392
and it's a corporate priority. It doesn't just sit with the Housing Services

10:11.496 --> 10:14.752
division. We've partnered directly with the

10:14.776 --> 10:18.416
team in PDLS and Build Waterloo Region. We also have

10:18.488 --> 10:22.496
incredible support from the rest of the corporation, whether that's facilities

10:22.608 --> 10:26.480
or legal or finance. That list goes on and on. with

10:26.520 --> 10:30.160
our current strategic plan and the strategic priority of homes for

10:30.200 --> 10:33.878
all, This work is a corporate priority and we continue to look

10:33.934 --> 10:37.414
at and use the entire energy of a corporation to

10:37.502 --> 10:41.750
accomplish this work and find new ways of doing so as

10:41.790 --> 10:44.770
well. Next slide, please.

10:46.430 --> 10:50.470
So in hitting our target about three and a half years into a five

10:50.510 --> 10:54.182
year initiative, our attention really turned to the question of

10:54.366 --> 10:58.664
where do we go from here? And so that started with an analysis

10:58.712 --> 11:02.200
of not only what we had accomplished, but how

11:02.320 --> 11:05.464
we had accomplished it. We then did engagement work, a whole lot

11:05.472 --> 11:09.144
of work with community, with area municipalities

11:09.192 --> 11:12.184
and others involved in this space, doing a lot of listening and getting a lot

11:12.192 --> 11:16.744
of feedback. And all of that is rolled up into the reports

11:16.912 --> 11:20.632
and plan that we have in front of you this morning.

11:20.816 --> 11:24.312
We've kept everything that worked well through its original

11:24.376 --> 11:27.924
version. We made improvements to what didn't, and we've incorporated

11:27.972 --> 11:32.052
new ideas, emerging and promising practices along the way

11:32.236 --> 11:35.732
as well. In addition to some

11:35.756 --> 11:39.800
of those new directions that we're going for, I want to highlight

11:40.860 --> 11:44.164
some changes in scope, both in terms of the quantum of what we're

11:44.212 --> 11:47.700
hoping to produce, but also for whom we want to create these

11:47.740 --> 11:51.652
homes for over the next five years. It was important to us as

11:51.676 --> 11:55.462
an internal staff team that we we not

11:55.486 --> 11:59.062
only continue to produce and support the development of new homes at

11:59.086 --> 12:02.998
the pace that we've been on, but to continue to find ways to push that

12:03.054 --> 12:06.390
pace even more. I spoke on the importance

12:06.470 --> 12:10.486
of this work to this community previously through our original

12:10.598 --> 12:14.022
vision for building better futures. While the environment that we

12:14.046 --> 12:18.070
find ourselves in today, this work is

12:18.110 --> 12:21.702
not getting easier, but it's getting more critical that we continue

12:21.806 --> 12:25.732
to find ways to push and go beyond

12:25.876 --> 12:29.396
what we've already achieved. So we don't want to just maintain the previous

12:29.468 --> 12:33.892
pace that we were on. We want to Push further and

12:33.916 --> 12:36.640
we want to push ourselves more to do this work.

12:36.940 --> 12:40.560
One of the ways that we think we can accomplish this is through

12:40.940 --> 12:44.436
looking at preservation of existing affordable housing.

12:44.548 --> 12:48.532
This was not a focus in the original iteration of this work.

12:48.716 --> 12:52.052
What we've seen over the last number of years in this country and in this

12:52.076 --> 12:56.038
province particularly, there's a lot of data coming out that's

12:56.054 --> 12:59.542
illustrating for every new unit that we're creating, we're losing

12:59.686 --> 13:03.110
some exponents more than what we're producing

13:03.190 --> 13:06.278
of the existing stock. So we want to pay

13:06.334 --> 13:10.810
particular attention to that area in this new iteration.

13:11.150 --> 13:14.566
We also think that given our role as service system manager,

13:14.758 --> 13:18.038
our connection to income support and public health

13:18.094 --> 13:22.110
programs, it's critical that we focus our efforts on the low to

13:22.150 --> 13:25.966
very low income households in this community. And you can see on this slide

13:26.158 --> 13:29.390
definitions of what we mean by those. So some

13:29.430 --> 13:32.782
change in scope in terms of continuing to push our

13:32.806 --> 13:36.414
efforts in this area to achieve even more and some narrowing

13:36.462 --> 13:40.370
in of scope on who we want to target these affordable units

13:40.790 --> 13:44.510
for overall. Next and last slide,

13:44.670 --> 13:47.902
please. So with this

13:48.006 --> 13:51.786
revised scope we've come up with a framework for the new actions

13:51.898 --> 13:55.386
to get us there. This includes continuing to incentivize

13:55.418 --> 13:59.242
affordable housing development, increasing overall community

13:59.346 --> 14:01.898
capacity to do this work and support this work,

14:02.034 --> 14:05.818
preserving existing affordable housing, further utilizing

14:05.834 --> 14:09.162
our Waterloo regional housing assets, disincentivizing the

14:09.186 --> 14:12.666
financialization of housing and continuing to refine and

14:12.738 --> 14:16.898
build on how we work together as a region, a regional corporation, and a regional

14:16.914 --> 14:20.146
community. Thanks for listening. Happy to take any questions

14:20.178 --> 14:21.230
you have on this work.

14:23.130 --> 14:24.790
Thank you. Councillor Shantz.

14:26.490 --> 14:29.554
Thank you. Thank you for the update. I was a

14:29.562 --> 14:33.218
little bit preoccupied trying to get my computer up and running while you were

14:33.274 --> 14:37.122
talking, but I, I think I got all of it. I just wanted to,

14:37.306 --> 14:40.590
to commend you and your staff on

14:41.530 --> 14:45.330
continuing to look at the issues and how

14:45.370 --> 14:48.822
you can address them Well and

14:48.846 --> 14:52.070
I particularly like the graphics. I think they tell the

14:52.110 --> 14:55.770
story really well. So the graphic with the continuum,

14:56.590 --> 15:00.182
the numbers that you've got on there are current

15:00.286 --> 15:03.718
numbers of

15:03.774 --> 15:06.050
housing I think, is that?

15:07.710 --> 15:09.730
Let me see if I can pull it up too.

15:10.670 --> 15:13.616
Go ahead. Sorry. Yeah,

15:13.808 --> 15:17.680
and, and so through you Chair and yeah, maybe we can get that

15:17.720 --> 15:21.056
slide back up. So those were,

15:21.128 --> 15:24.096
I think towards the end of, end of the year, those were current. There's probably

15:24.128 --> 15:27.020
some slight adjustments Councillor, that we could make.

15:27.960 --> 15:32.064
There's some old census data that's on there as well, but

15:32.112 --> 15:35.856
they're, they're what I would call reasonably current overall.

15:36.048 --> 15:39.212
So I mean, obviously you're, you have

15:39.236 --> 15:44.428
been looking at the data and the need is in supportive

15:44.604 --> 15:47.660
housing, particularly if it's low and very

15:47.700 --> 15:51.468
low, Or where do those that

15:51.524 --> 15:54.480
focus, where does that focus fit on this graph?

15:56.660 --> 15:59.772
Thank you. And through you, Chair, So when

15:59.796 --> 16:02.988
it comes to supporting the work of the plan to end chronic homelessness,

16:03.004 --> 16:06.960
and if we're going to be successful in that work overall,

16:07.660 --> 16:11.320
supportive housing is critical to those efforts.

16:12.300 --> 16:15.508
It's not needed by everybody that's experiencing homelessness and it's not needed

16:15.524 --> 16:19.092
by the majority of people long term necessarily either. But it's a critical

16:19.156 --> 16:22.292
step and it's an area that we want to focus on particular

16:22.396 --> 16:26.356
for that population. When it comes to low and

16:26.428 --> 16:29.492
very low incomes across the region,

16:29.676 --> 16:33.332
that's not just people that are homeless either. It's people

16:33.436 --> 16:36.930
that are struggling with where they're at right

16:36.970 --> 16:39.954
now in their ability to pay their housing costs.

16:40.002 --> 16:43.730
So it's supportive housing for sure to

16:43.770 --> 16:47.474
help us be successful in the plan to end chronic homelessness.

16:47.602 --> 16:51.186
But community housing and affordable rental housing

16:51.298 --> 16:55.234
are also really critical components for that low and very low income

16:55.282 --> 16:59.170
group that just needs support in their ability to pay the

16:59.210 --> 17:01.872
rent overall.

17:01.936 --> 17:05.136
So it's a group that would span that range

17:05.248 --> 17:08.496
of the housing continuum overall in those various different built forms

17:08.528 --> 17:10.140
of housing that assist.

17:12.680 --> 17:17.460
If I can push a little more just to understand the quantum.

17:18.120 --> 17:21.456
If you were to take, So this is what we have,

17:21.608 --> 17:25.312
If you were to take the situation where I mean,

17:25.336 --> 17:28.688
we're saying we've got 2,500 homeless people, if you were to take the

17:28.744 --> 17:32.560
actual situation and put numbers in

17:32.600 --> 17:36.528
there, where are we the most

17:36.584 --> 17:39.744
lacking? And I'm assuming I know the answer because of your report,

17:39.832 --> 17:43.936
but. thank

17:43.968 --> 17:46.700
you. That is the billion dollar question,

17:47.080 --> 17:50.512
Councillor. I think the stat

17:50.616 --> 17:54.160
that best illustrates the need overall,

17:54.320 --> 17:57.696
it's not the wait list or it's not the number of people that are currently

17:57.768 --> 18:01.100
homeless, it's core housing need. When we look at core housing need,

18:01.140 --> 18:04.572
core housing need is a measure of people that

18:04.596 --> 18:08.524
are paying more than 30% of their income on

18:08.532 --> 18:12.124
their gross income on their current housing situation. That's a

18:12.132 --> 18:16.284
population that's struggling to pay their housing costs on a daily basis

18:16.332 --> 18:20.316
and are most at risk of becoming homeless or entering

18:20.348 --> 18:23.788
the system one way or another. When we look at census data

18:23.844 --> 18:27.686
such as we have, that's about 20,000 individuals across

18:27.758 --> 18:30.370
this region. It's a staggering number.

18:30.830 --> 18:33.942
Wherever we invest along the continuum, it's going

18:33.966 --> 18:37.846
to help out, it's going to create more capacity and I think the sweet

18:37.878 --> 18:41.046
spot for us in this work, both through the plan to end chronic

18:41.078 --> 18:44.342
homelessness and through the work of building Better Futures is

18:44.366 --> 18:48.486
on the continuum between supportive housing and affordable rental housing.

18:48.678 --> 18:52.170
That's where we need to be putting our efforts. that will free up

18:52.290 --> 18:57.082
additional capacity and need and demand for

18:57.186 --> 19:00.666
our waterloo regional housing stock. It'll help prevent people from entering

19:00.698 --> 19:04.666
into homelessness to begin with. It'll help them exit homelessness

19:04.698 --> 19:08.346
overall. So if we can target that area of the continuum as we're pointing

19:08.378 --> 19:12.570
to, in these efforts and how our plans work hand in glove together,

19:12.690 --> 19:15.270
that's where we should be putting our energy and our investment.

19:16.220 --> 19:19.956
Okay, can I ask one more? Thank you. So,

19:20.028 --> 19:24.180
So there's two pieces to that. There's one, if

19:24.220 --> 19:28.080
you've got people that can't afford their rent, but maybe have a space,

19:28.460 --> 19:32.932
So that's where we provide, we may provide some

19:33.116 --> 19:36.852
rental support help. And then there's the

19:36.876 --> 19:40.276
lack of actual rental space. How do you,

19:40.348 --> 19:42.160
how do you balance those two?

19:46.650 --> 19:50.642
Yeah, thank you. Thank you again. So, yeah, it's a

19:50.666 --> 19:54.562
constant balancing act. So as you've seen through this

19:54.586 --> 19:58.242
work of our WRH revitalization plan, we're taking some

19:58.266 --> 20:01.990
ownership and some leadership in building the spaces ourselves,

20:02.330 --> 20:06.322
which is needed and necessary for

20:06.346 --> 20:10.304
the population also for a sustainability and longevity

20:10.352 --> 20:14.528
standpoint as well. They'll remain in the system as intended,

20:14.704 --> 20:18.592
in perpetuity That way. we balance some of those efforts and the cost

20:18.696 --> 20:22.720
that's associated with doing that work out with things like rent supplements.

20:22.800 --> 20:26.416
So those are spaces that already exist out in the community

20:26.568 --> 20:30.740
in the form of private or not for profit landlords.

20:31.080 --> 20:34.752
And then we're able to assist somebody either in their current situation or

20:34.776 --> 20:38.188
to move into that situation by paying the difference between

20:38.244 --> 20:42.812
what they can afford and what the actual rent is at

20:42.836 --> 20:46.524
those spaces. So it's a balance between supporting

20:46.572 --> 20:49.852
the creation of new spaces and also utilizing what's out there

20:49.876 --> 20:53.564
in the market right now to the best of our ability and operating

20:53.612 --> 20:57.372
on both of those fronts. Thank you and thanks again for

20:57.396 --> 21:00.720
all the work you're doing. Councillor McCabe.

21:01.060 --> 21:04.920
Thank you. And just want to echo what Councillor Shantz said about

21:05.570 --> 21:09.274
congratulations on hitting the marks early and being ambitious

21:09.322 --> 21:12.826
with the next go. I think that really speaks volumes

21:12.858 --> 21:16.522
for the work that you're doing and the partnerships that you've developed. So thank you

21:16.546 --> 21:19.386
for that. I have a few questions.

21:19.578 --> 21:21.990
One of them was just to understand,

21:23.570 --> 21:27.898
There's a paragraph about municipal right of refusal policies

21:28.074 --> 21:31.482
and I just wanted to make sure I understand that. Does it mean that without

21:31.586 --> 21:34.924
provincial legislation, municipalities can't purchase

21:34.972 --> 21:38.620
a single family home if it's bidding against like

21:38.660 --> 21:42.492
an individual who's trying to purchase a home. Or does

21:42.516 --> 21:46.480
it mean that without the provincial legislation

21:46.820 --> 21:50.556
a municipality isn't automatically

21:50.668 --> 21:53.756
the successful purchaser? There's a paragraph, I forget what,

21:53.828 --> 21:57.404
I didn't mark what page it's on, but you know what I'm referring

21:57.452 --> 22:00.892
to? Go ahead.  Yeah, thank you.

22:00.916 --> 22:04.092
And through the chair, it's closer to the

22:04.116 --> 22:07.336
latter in your, in your question,

22:07.488 --> 22:10.872
Councillor McCabe. So this is a policy that

22:10.896 --> 22:14.616
exists elsewhere in the country, Quebec as one example.

22:14.728 --> 22:17.180
And what it means is that when a property,

22:17.600 --> 22:21.272
if the province was to enable the legislation here, it would

22:21.296 --> 22:25.464
allow that when either an affordable or otherwise

22:25.592 --> 22:29.064
property came up for sale, the municipality would get the

22:29.072 --> 22:32.282
first shot at purchasing it for

22:32.306 --> 22:36.954
the fair market value of the property. The municipality

22:37.002 --> 22:40.910
could take that or could decline that opportunity,

22:41.330 --> 22:45.338
depending. But it would give us, It would give the municipality

22:45.514 --> 22:48.906
the first right to preserve housing stock

22:48.938 --> 22:52.682
or to take on additional stock That happens to be for sale. And that

22:52.706 --> 22:55.590
could be any type of, like a single family home,

22:56.130 --> 22:59.114
three storey apartment. Like it could be any kind of. Yeah,

22:59.162 --> 23:02.506
Depending on what the province would choose to enable.

23:02.618 --> 23:06.170
They would have to take the legislation and spell that out specifically.

23:06.670 --> 23:10.022
They could target it to various built forms, I suppose,

23:10.086 --> 23:13.078
but that opportunity would be there.

23:13.134 --> 23:16.742
So first step would be the province would have to enable that and

23:16.766 --> 23:20.022
then it's, If supported, then it

23:20.126 --> 23:24.050
would come to the area municipal level to

23:25.870 --> 23:29.382
follow that up or not. So it's a policy that we've been watching.

23:29.446 --> 23:32.022
It's one that we're interested in. We think it has a lot of benefit in

23:32.046 --> 23:35.608
terms of preservation of existing units

23:35.784 --> 23:39.704
in particular. And we've seen where other countries have engaged

23:39.752 --> 23:43.180
in this type of work and affordable housing is more successful.

23:44.240 --> 23:48.264
It's policies like this that help that and eliminate some

23:48.272 --> 23:51.400
of the competition, bidding wars and price

23:51.520 --> 23:55.112
escalations that happen when properties go up for sale elsewhere. And so have

23:55.136 --> 23:58.900
you had conversations with ministry staff about this?

23:59.350 --> 24:02.766
And I'm just wondering, do you have a sense of where

24:02.838 --> 24:06.366
this may or may not be in the, I know we just had an election

24:06.438 --> 24:10.398
and that, but is this something that we've put forward and we've

24:10.414 --> 24:13.646
heard a hard no. Or are they open to hearing about

24:13.718 --> 24:17.210
this, or do we know. Go ahead.

24:17.510 --> 24:20.798
Through you chair, I'd say at this point we don't really know.

24:20.854 --> 24:24.782
It's been, it's been interesting times with

24:24.806 --> 24:28.622
the ministries lately. There are conversations that, that we're open to

24:28.646 --> 24:32.186
and it's something that we'd like to pursue either through a staff level or even

24:32.258 --> 24:35.210
a council advocacy level in the, in the coming years as part of this work

24:35.250 --> 24:38.602
to understand what the appetite might be for something like this. Okay, yeah,

24:38.666 --> 24:42.218
that's okay. That's good to know. That's really good to know. My other question

24:42.274 --> 24:46.586
is, what do you mean by the deincentivization of the financialization

24:46.698 --> 24:50.202
of housing? I mean, in terms of, I know what the financialization

24:50.266 --> 24:53.482
of housing means and what's happening, but what is it

24:53.506 --> 24:57.050
that you are like thinking that we

24:57.090 --> 25:00.630
can do or to address that?

25:01.100 --> 25:04.760
Go ahead. because that's a big challenge.

25:05.260 --> 25:08.880
It absolutely is. And it's one that we think that

25:09.260 --> 25:13.316
we can have a role in both again through advocacy and

25:13.468 --> 25:17.076
direct policy implementations ourselves. So the initiative

25:17.108 --> 25:20.996
that we were just speaking of, for example, that would help contribute

25:21.028 --> 25:24.996
to the deincentivization of financialization

25:25.108 --> 25:28.798
of housing overall. Also, the more that we are

25:28.854 --> 25:32.850
able to maintain and retain

25:33.750 --> 25:37.694
housing stock ourselves as regional or not for profit entities,

25:37.822 --> 25:41.150
that also further contributes to that work. And again,

25:41.190 --> 25:44.462
where we've seen countries that have really embraced

25:44.606 --> 25:48.718
affordable housing, we see those levels of government really

25:48.774 --> 25:53.414
playing a strong role in ownership and retention of

25:53.422 --> 25:56.886
the community housing stock. Okay, thank you. And I had a couple other questions,

25:56.918 --> 25:59.702
but I don't know if anyone else was in the queue. There's no one else

25:59.766 --> 26:02.950
in the queue, so you're on. Keep going. Okay,

26:03.110 --> 26:06.950
thank you. So I

26:06.990 --> 26:10.582
also wanted to ask a question about rent geared to income

26:10.646 --> 26:14.038
and the process by which. So yesterday

26:14.214 --> 26:18.410
with the MP in Waterloo, we had a meeting with a variety of people

26:18.450 --> 26:22.170
who live in, some of them, about half of them

26:22.210 --> 26:25.546
live in region of Waterloo housing,

26:25.658 --> 26:29.470
and then others live in privately owned

26:30.450 --> 26:34.670
apartments or townhouses. Not great situations.

26:35.490 --> 26:39.002
But one of the things they asked was, because some

26:39.026 --> 26:43.034
of them are living with rent geared to income, but how they

26:43.122 --> 26:46.602
explained it to me was that for them, the incentive to

26:46.626 --> 26:50.372
go in some cases get a job or improve, you know,

26:50.396 --> 26:54.004
or take a promotion or get a, you know, a higher paying job

26:54.092 --> 26:57.240
is offset by the fact that their rent,

26:57.660 --> 27:01.480
whoops, their rent will immediately start to rise

27:01.900 --> 27:04.760
and doesn't allow them the opportunity to save,

27:05.580 --> 27:08.980
in order to save money to either make a down

27:09.020 --> 27:12.596
payment on a house or a down payment

27:12.628 --> 27:16.862
for a better apartment or what have you. So is

27:16.886 --> 27:20.062
it within our purview as regional council,

27:20.166 --> 27:23.230
like from a policy perspective that we could take a look at that

27:23.270 --> 27:26.590
and say, you know, if you're living, if that's the situation that

27:26.630 --> 27:29.950
we would allow you, I don't know what the time frame would be, six months

27:29.990 --> 27:33.518
to A year, at the same, paying the same

27:33.574 --> 27:36.862
rent to allow someone the opportunity to

27:36.886 --> 27:40.970
do that. Or is that, is that legislated again by

27:41.780 --> 27:45.084
provincial legislation? Ryan?

27:45.172 --> 27:48.412
Or have we tried that and it doesn't work? And just tell me

27:48.436 --> 27:51.500
what's. Yeah, curious about that. Yeah,

27:51.660 --> 27:55.324
thanks for the question, Question again, Councillor. It's, it's a

27:55.332 --> 27:58.812
growing concern. It has been a significant change

27:58.916 --> 28:02.572
in the overall rent geared to income housing stock over the last last decade or

28:02.596 --> 28:05.788
so. It was never necessarily intended to be a

28:05.924 --> 28:09.888
A lifetime sort of situation for, for individuals,

28:10.044 --> 28:13.896
but meant to stabilize them until their income

28:13.928 --> 28:17.576
situation improved and then move on to market

28:17.648 --> 28:21.032
housing. What we've seen in the market over the

28:21.056 --> 28:25.112
last 5 to 10 years is that even when people's situations

28:25.176 --> 28:29.384
stabilize and their income improves, there's still nowhere

28:29.432 --> 28:33.464
for them to move in terms of affordability after rent

28:33.512 --> 28:37.304
geared to income housing. And that's created a lot of the backlog

28:37.352 --> 28:41.664
across that housing continuum that ultimately winds up in the significant

28:41.752 --> 28:45.980
homelessness challenges that we're all seeing. We have no,

28:46.520 --> 28:50.384
The whole calculation process and rules that govern how

28:50.392 --> 28:54.432
we operate rent geared to income housing is legislated by

28:54.456 --> 28:58.448
the province both in terms of access. We have no ability

28:58.544 --> 29:01.904
to force somebody to move on.

29:02.072 --> 29:05.712
What we do have the ability to do is to provide programs and

29:05.736 --> 29:08.880
incentives that, that help with that work too.

29:09.180 --> 29:12.724
We created an enhanced homeownership

29:12.772 --> 29:16.020
program a number of years ago to try to do just that.

29:16.060 --> 29:19.332
We partnered with one of the local credit unions as well

29:19.356 --> 29:22.692
as our own homeownership program and try to work with our

29:22.716 --> 29:26.708
tenants that have gained that level of income to help support

29:26.764 --> 29:29.540
them into what comes next.

29:29.660 --> 29:33.572
We've had varying degrees of success with, with that

29:33.596 --> 29:36.996
approach, but I think that that or other program

29:37.068 --> 29:41.156
or policy changes that we can make to incentivize and support somebody

29:41.188 --> 29:44.884
to your example into that next step would help with that flow

29:44.932 --> 29:48.132
through and create better positive movement through

29:48.156 --> 29:51.332
the housing continuum that we see currently. So that's where

29:51.356 --> 29:54.840
we have the ability to play or to make changes

29:55.260 --> 29:59.076
is really with that support and incentivization pieces to

29:59.148 --> 30:02.348
help people that are ready to move to what's next. Okay, thank you. I wasn't

30:02.364 --> 30:06.108
aware of that enhanced homeownership piece, so I'll share that with them.

30:06.244 --> 30:09.564
I have some other people in the queue now, Councillor. Okay, can I just finish

30:09.612 --> 30:13.052
this question and then I'll go back in? Okay. so if

30:13.076 --> 30:17.196
I understood you correctly, if someone does get

30:17.348 --> 30:20.572
a job with an increased salary, we don't have a leverage

30:20.636 --> 30:26.076
to tell them to move out anyway. Is that what you said? essentially,

30:26.108 --> 30:29.766
correct. So when they're in a rent geared income situation right now,

30:29.868 --> 30:33.858
say paying through their social assistance, say they

30:33.914 --> 30:36.670
gain employment and are now working,

30:37.050 --> 30:41.122
we would shift their rent so that it's now 30% of their new

30:41.146 --> 30:44.898
income source up to a maximum cap. We call that market

30:44.954 --> 30:48.670
rent. It's not actually equivalent to the market. It's also

30:50.330 --> 30:53.442
deflated by provincial legislation as well. But they would

30:53.466 --> 30:56.594
be paying the maximum rent that they can in that rent geared to

30:56.642 --> 30:59.812
income situation and no, we have no ability

30:59.876 --> 31:03.812
beyond that to force them to move on from

31:03.836 --> 31:07.732
there. Councillor James. thank you. I just want to thank staff for

31:07.756 --> 31:11.396
the report on this and being so thorough and

31:11.468 --> 31:15.640
all of the efforts happening. I would like to move this motion

31:16.300 --> 31:21.732
and Councillor Foxton will second that. moved

31:21.756 --> 31:25.394
by Councillor James. Seconded by Councillor Foxton. Do you

31:25.402 --> 31:28.722
have some comments now, Councillor James? Nope, that's all I

31:28.746 --> 31:32.402
wanted to. okay. Can I call the question. Well there's still

31:32.426 --> 31:36.194
speakers in the queue, so I don't. I do have a couple other people of

31:36.202 --> 31:38.390
the queue here to speak now. Councillor Huinink,

31:39.210 --> 31:42.434
Chair Erb I'm glad the motion is on the table and I'm glad it's going to

31:42.442 --> 31:45.826
be seconded. And I just wanted to clarify

31:45.938 --> 31:50.082
something that Councillor McCabe asked about.

31:50.266 --> 31:53.514
When, when your income goes up and your rent goes up.

31:53.642 --> 31:57.466
That's true, but it is that a maximum of 30%,

31:57.618 --> 32:01.802
Is that correct, Mr. Pettipierre? So regardless of how

32:01.826 --> 32:05.482
much you make, the ceiling of your

32:05.506 --> 32:10.350
rent payment in rent geared to income is 30%, correct?

32:12.450 --> 32:15.946
through you, Mr. Chair, You're correct, Councillor. So all of our rent geared to

32:15.978 --> 32:19.962
income units, people are not paying more than 30% of their

32:19.986 --> 32:23.934
income at any point in time. So there is still incentive

32:24.062 --> 32:27.566
for people to get better employment when they can.

32:27.718 --> 32:31.310
It's just not as much incentive as

32:31.430 --> 32:35.726
if we offered a break. But it's still a fairly good incentive

32:35.838 --> 32:37.810
in my opinion. Thanks.

32:39.910 --> 32:43.490
Okay. And Oh and just one final. Oh, sorry.

32:44.470 --> 32:47.582
Okay. You're recognized. Okay, thank you.

32:47.686 --> 32:51.116
Just., I can't let a discussion about building

32:51.188 --> 32:55.020
better futures go by without asking about 555 Beechwood.

32:55.100 --> 32:58.076
I just want to, My understanding is it's with the city,

32:58.148 --> 33:01.068
but once that's done we're ready to move on it,

33:01.124 --> 33:04.876
Correct. So that'll? correct,

33:04.908 --> 33:08.012
Councillor. There's a few agreements with, with the city right

33:08.036 --> 33:11.420
now we're waiting to be finalized and if

33:11.460 --> 33:15.222
those are done soon we can, we can keep moving to

33:15.406 --> 33:19.318
the next steps which would be final site plan approval and

33:19.374 --> 33:22.902
hopefully a building permit. Yeah, okay. That would be great. And just I do want

33:22.926 --> 33:26.454
to put on the record that again, at this conversation that I had with these

33:26.622 --> 33:29.130
individuals at Sunnydale yesterday,

33:29.790 --> 33:33.926
we need to take a look at the state of regional

33:33.958 --> 33:37.650
housing, particularly in that area, because some of the

33:37.950 --> 33:41.410
issues that they raised were

33:41.450 --> 33:45.490
quite shocking. So I have followed up with Councillor Erb about

33:45.530 --> 33:49.042
that. But I do want to put on the record that we do need

33:49.066 --> 33:52.146
to take a look at that because there's a wide variety

33:52.178 --> 33:55.730
of outstanding issues that were really quite shocking to hear about

33:55.770 --> 33:57.710
from the tenants. Thank you.

33:58.650 --> 34:02.590
Okay, we have a motion, a mover and a seconder, and

34:02.890 --> 34:04.950
I don't see any other hands raised.

34:07.190 --> 34:11.134
When Building Better Futures came to council in 21 with

34:11.222 --> 34:14.414
a target of 2,500 homes in five years, it was sort of,

34:14.502 --> 34:17.758
Oh, wow, that would be wonderful. Well, here we are coming

34:17.814 --> 34:21.790
back now with 3,500. So it's another Wow and

34:21.830 --> 34:25.822
more wonderful. So Great work for the innovation and

34:25.846 --> 34:28.990
the strategic alliances that hopefully we're going to be able to build

34:29.030 --> 34:32.318
to make this work, and particularly for the focus

34:32.374 --> 34:35.492
on the low and low income people in our community that we're able to

34:35.516 --> 34:39.620
maybe start making some progress there. So great work

34:39.660 --> 34:42.692
and thanks for, you know, pushing this thing

34:42.716 --> 34:46.720
out, having the vision to really make this something worthwhile in our community.

34:48.860 --> 34:51.640
Would you use your eScribe to vote.

34:52.300 --> 34:54.320
My clerk wants me to move on.

34:59.670 --> 35:02.130
I'm waiting for my queue,

35:02.790 --> 35:04.650
I'm in favor of it.

