01:05:49.878 --> 01:05:53.406
years wait list for supportive mental

01:05:53.438 --> 01:05:57.054
health and 253 days for voluntary treatment

01:05:57.102 --> 01:06:01.666
for drug addiction. So 12 isn't

01:06:01.698 --> 01:06:05.330
going to cut it. What are your greatest fears

01:06:05.410 --> 01:06:07.310
during this gap period?

01:06:10.730 --> 01:06:14.110
Through you, the chair, the gap period between

01:06:14.890 --> 01:06:18.706
the housing or, can I clarify? the end of the consumption,

01:06:18.898 --> 01:06:22.110
the CTS and when

01:06:23.210 --> 01:06:26.852
all of these beds and things are going to happen. Yeah.

01:06:26.916 --> 01:06:30.212
So we are working with the, As we wind down

01:06:30.236 --> 01:06:34.532
the CTS, the HART Hub is winding up at the same time and

01:06:34.556 --> 01:06:38.612
we do have a number of different mitigation strategies in place to look

01:06:38.636 --> 01:06:42.000
at what gaps there are in terms of the harm reduction services.

01:06:42.300 --> 01:06:46.084
We did mention some of those already. Is there

01:06:46.092 --> 01:06:49.444
anything, Dr. Wang, you'd like to add? Yeah. And just for

01:06:49.452 --> 01:06:52.800
your awareness, Appendix A lists them in more detail.

01:06:54.230 --> 01:06:57.770
The actions that we'll be taking with partners to try to,

01:06:58.790 --> 01:07:01.770
you know, reduce to the extent possible, of course,

01:07:02.550 --> 01:07:06.610
the effects of no longer having a CTS.

01:07:07.430 --> 01:07:08.210
Thanks.

01:07:10.870 --> 01:07:14.686
Okay. Anything else?

01:07:14.878 --> 01:07:17.966
Yeah, I was just going to say through you, the chair, obviously our biggest fear

01:07:17.998 --> 01:07:21.512
is, is people overdosing from toxic drug supply.

01:07:21.656 --> 01:07:25.224
I mean, I think that goes without saying. And HART Hub does not

01:07:25.232 --> 01:07:28.584
replace the CTS. You're correct. So we're

01:07:28.632 --> 01:07:31.992
attempting to build capacity so that people know the places they can go

01:07:32.016 --> 01:07:35.272
for supports. And I know we are going

01:07:35.296 --> 01:07:38.920
to have 12 transitional beds. That's not the only service provided in

01:07:38.960 --> 01:07:42.904
HART Hub, Right. So the teams that are already in those places

01:07:42.952 --> 01:07:47.460
where we know folks are accessing toxic drug supply will

01:07:47.500 --> 01:07:51.220
be larger, will be more present, will be in more locations and

01:07:51.260 --> 01:07:55.044
will have additional expertise in addictions and mental health so

01:07:55.052 --> 01:07:58.932
that if they can't access a bed, they're still going to be able to access

01:07:59.036 --> 01:08:01.480
services through a variety of different ways.

01:08:02.620 --> 01:08:05.716
The 12 beds, you're right, probably will not be enough.

01:08:05.868 --> 01:08:09.156
But what we heard from the community is that we don't

01:08:09.188 --> 01:08:12.398
have beds where people can go who are interested in recovery,

01:08:12.524 --> 01:08:15.706
and we don't have beds where people can go who are coming out of recovery,

01:08:15.738 --> 01:08:19.146
and then be successful in maintaining their abstinence. So that's

01:08:19.178 --> 01:08:23.110
what those beds are targeting. Thank you.

01:08:24.530 --> 01:08:28.362
Councillor Craig. Well, thank you,

01:08:28.386 --> 01:08:31.270
Mr. Chair, and I appreciate the presentation today.

01:08:31.730 --> 01:08:35.750
My concerns, and perhaps Dr. Wang could help me on this

01:08:36.130 --> 01:08:39.840
is. It's more of a direct criticism than anything in terms

01:08:39.880 --> 01:08:42.656
of an observation, but we have,

01:08:42.808 --> 01:08:46.944
coming in 12 beds, we have over 10,000 people

01:08:47.032 --> 01:08:50.060
who are accessing drug paraphernalia in the region.

01:08:50.360 --> 01:08:53.584
We have about 2,000-3,000 using drugs daily.

01:08:53.712 --> 01:08:57.200
How does this in any way mitigate what's going

01:08:57.240 --> 01:09:01.040
on? It seems to me to be totally inadequate.

01:09:01.200 --> 01:09:03.840
And that's not a criticism of the concept,

01:09:04.000 --> 01:09:07.449
it's a criticism of the size and the

01:09:07.489 --> 01:09:11.389
functionality of it in terms of its limitations financially.

01:09:14.209 --> 01:09:19.881
Thank you for the question, Councillor Craig. Yes. So ourselves,

01:09:19.945 --> 01:09:24.793
along with our community partners, are putting

01:09:24.841 --> 01:09:29.449
in place programs and services which have been authorized

01:09:29.609 --> 01:09:33.251
and funded or cost shared in some circumstances

01:09:33.425 --> 01:09:37.088
with the province that they believe

01:09:37.144 --> 01:09:40.512
is the right action or actions to take in order to

01:09:40.536 --> 01:09:44.256
try to address the opioid

01:09:44.288 --> 01:09:48.060
crisis which we know is growing. And our community

01:09:48.360 --> 01:09:52.480
is not alone in that This is an issue that all

01:09:52.520 --> 01:09:55.584
communities in Ontario and Canada are grappling

01:09:55.632 --> 01:10:00.078
with. And so government has put in

01:10:00.134 --> 01:10:03.790
place various initiatives. Previously, one of those was

01:10:03.830 --> 01:10:07.530
a CTS and there's been a change in direction. And now

01:10:08.230 --> 01:10:13.294
they have decided to authorize

01:10:13.422 --> 01:10:17.102
and fund what's known as the

01:10:17.126 --> 01:10:20.590
HART Hub model, which includes all the services that

01:10:20.630 --> 01:10:24.126
our presenters mentioned. And with a certain

01:10:24.198 --> 01:10:27.820
percentage of that funding going to affordable housing,

01:10:27.980 --> 01:10:31.884
sorry, supportive housing. So it is actually the

01:10:31.892 --> 01:10:35.660
government that determines the funding pot and the types of

01:10:35.700 --> 01:10:39.644
services that will be allowed and funded. So our role

01:10:39.812 --> 01:10:44.060
here, along with the partners in our community, is to try to take

01:10:44.100 --> 01:10:48.012
advantage of these funding opportunities and get

01:10:48.036 --> 01:10:51.436
as much funding as we can, put in place programs and services

01:10:51.508 --> 01:10:56.140
that we think within the parameters of those

01:10:56.520 --> 01:11:00.540
authorized programs that we think will provide the best

01:11:01.160 --> 01:11:04.832
results for the money that will be given.

01:11:05.016 --> 01:11:08.976
And so that's what we've presented

01:11:09.008 --> 01:11:10.780
here to council today.

01:11:11.800 --> 01:11:15.500
Understanding that it is a very

01:11:15.800 --> 01:11:18.944
difficult challenge, the drug addiction

01:11:18.992 --> 01:11:23.126
and overdose challenge that we face in our community. And there are also

01:11:23.198 --> 01:11:27.238
many other services that are not necessarily receiving

01:11:27.414 --> 01:11:31.670
provincial funding that still play an important role in continuing to try

01:11:31.790 --> 01:11:33.610
to address These challenges,

01:11:34.910 --> 01:11:39.078
and you know, they're not, either not

01:11:39.214 --> 01:11:42.950
covered by the province, but could have other funding sources,

01:11:43.030 --> 01:11:47.232
community funding sources, for example, or sometimes federal funding sources like

01:11:47.256 --> 01:11:51.040
our harm reduction partners provide. There's also other services that

01:11:51.080 --> 01:11:54.224
the province provides, but not through

01:11:54.312 --> 01:11:57.392
ourselves. Well, in this case,

01:11:57.496 --> 01:12:01.040
it's still not to ourselves. CTS funding was through us, but HART

01:12:01.080 --> 01:12:04.720
hub funding will not be through the region or public health. It will be

01:12:04.760 --> 01:12:07.060
from the province to the HART hubs. So

01:12:08.040 --> 01:12:10.700
For example, the mental health and addiction services,

01:12:11.000 --> 01:12:15.030
they're funded separately through, through the ministry

01:12:15.190 --> 01:12:18.822
to the partners. So there's, there's a, so this

01:12:18.846 --> 01:12:22.170
is one type of intervention,

01:12:23.150 --> 01:12:26.758
or I should say a suite of programs and services that,

01:12:26.814 --> 01:12:29.910
you know, we had the opportunity to apply for and

01:12:29.950 --> 01:12:34.326
fortunately we got, and we think this will help

01:12:34.398 --> 01:12:38.246
address the opioid challenge that we face.

01:12:38.398 --> 01:12:41.550
Thank you. If I may, Mr. Chair,

01:12:42.970 --> 01:12:46.402
would you not agree that what we're doing,

01:12:46.586 --> 01:12:50.770
what we've been provided with, falls far short of

01:12:50.810 --> 01:12:54.674
what we need to do in this region in terms of dealing with

01:12:54.762 --> 01:12:59.058
mental health, addiction and everything else? This seems minuscule

01:12:59.154 --> 01:13:01.870
in terms of an approach. That's all I'm saying.

01:13:03.290 --> 01:13:06.498
For sure. I'm putting you on the spot. I don't mean to do that,

01:13:06.554 --> 01:13:10.382
but I'm just, I have to ask this because it just seems far out

01:13:10.406 --> 01:13:11.490
of proportion.

01:13:13.510 --> 01:13:16.830
Yes, for sure. I mean, I think, you know, I think many

01:13:16.870 --> 01:13:19.810
would agree with your comment that there's,

01:13:20.390 --> 01:13:23.790
there's much more need than there are resources at this

01:13:23.830 --> 01:13:27.966
time. You know, what action we can take

01:13:28.118 --> 01:13:31.438
with the, with the parameters we've been given

01:13:31.574 --> 01:13:35.136
is to do the very best job that we can and to demonstrate

01:13:35.168 --> 01:13:38.320
to this government that their investment of services in our

01:13:38.360 --> 01:13:42.352
region was the right move and

01:13:42.456 --> 01:13:46.460
hopefully continue to build on that to advocate for more funding in the future.

01:13:47.240 --> 01:13:49.580
I appreciate your comments. Thank you.

01:13:52.120 --> 01:13:55.840
I think what we're doing is the best we can with the resources that

01:13:55.960 --> 01:13:59.754
the province has allocated to us. And the region of Waterloo is

01:13:59.762 --> 01:14:02.830
not unique to any other municipality in the province of Ontario.

