02:04:09.126 --> 02:04:12.606
the HART Hub. We are actively engaged with their operations committee

02:04:12.758 --> 02:04:16.158
And the work that House of Friendship and Community

02:04:16.214 --> 02:04:19.550
Health Care KW and Public Health are doing is really instrumental to us

02:04:19.590 --> 02:04:22.846
moving the needle on our responses to those calls.

02:04:23.038 --> 02:04:26.542
So I would like to say thank you for that to them. You'll see

02:04:26.566 --> 02:04:30.430
that if you've had a chance to read the reports, vehicles responses

02:04:30.510 --> 02:04:33.370
increased compared to 2023,

02:04:34.230 --> 02:04:37.390
even though there was an increase in the number of vehicle responses, There was

02:04:37.430 --> 02:04:41.262
a decrease in the unit utilization, which is obviously an indicator

02:04:41.326 --> 02:04:44.590
that the investments that Council has made and other strategies,

02:04:44.670 --> 02:04:48.184
mitigation strategies we are doing is, is working, the combination

02:04:48.232 --> 02:04:52.264
of those two things. We have seen an overall

02:04:52.312 --> 02:04:56.280
response time improvement relative to 2023 and

02:04:56.320 --> 02:04:59.784
probably one of the biggest things we're really pleased about is the ambulance offload

02:04:59.832 --> 02:05:02.920
time, significant improvement and I'll get to that in a future slide. But it is

02:05:02.960 --> 02:05:06.440
a, has been a real win in the last 12

02:05:06.480 --> 02:05:09.800
months. As you will remember in the fall we came

02:05:09.840 --> 02:05:13.416
to you with, telling you we were changing away from Code Reds and Code

02:05:13.448 --> 02:05:16.968
Yellows, changing them to new terms and

02:05:17.104 --> 02:05:20.856
Code Zero, which was formerly Code Red, We've seen improvements on, and Code Critical,

02:05:20.888 --> 02:05:24.696
We're still analyzing partly because under Code

02:05:24.728 --> 02:05:28.568
Yellow it was three ambulances or less available and we changed

02:05:28.584 --> 02:05:31.752
that threshold in order to apply mitigation strategies. So the

02:05:31.776 --> 02:05:35.832
analysis is still ongoing there. But you'll see that in many of the areas we

02:05:35.856 --> 02:05:39.230
have an improvement in everything to do with resource availability,

02:05:39.310 --> 02:05:41.450
which is very helpful. Next slide please.

02:05:42.630 --> 02:05:46.702
Getting into it in a bit more detail, we've seen

02:05:46.726 --> 02:05:50.222
a 2.7% increase compared to 2023 in terms of

02:05:50.246 --> 02:05:53.742
vehicle responses. I will note for Council that these are

02:05:53.766 --> 02:05:57.310
the vehicle responses to all the 911 calls we respond to.

02:05:57.430 --> 02:06:01.342
So in terms of unique identifying, unique 911 calls we respond

02:06:01.406 --> 02:06:04.824
to for 2024 there was just under 54,000.

02:06:04.942 --> 02:06:07.460
So that shows you that on a number of calls we're sending more than one

02:06:07.500 --> 02:06:11.284
resource based on what's needed. But the both numbers

02:06:11.332 --> 02:06:14.480
together kind of work in conjunction to tell us about system health.

02:06:15.180 --> 02:06:19.012
So about 22,000 more vehicle responses. And our projections,

02:06:19.076 --> 02:06:22.660
and these are projections, it's not just based on a single year over year,

02:06:22.700 --> 02:06:26.052
it's over time, a defined time period of

02:06:26.076 --> 02:06:29.652
a number of years. By 2026, we are expecting that

02:06:29.676 --> 02:06:33.196
if nothing changes, we could hit the 83,000 vehicle

02:06:33.228 --> 02:06:36.440
response mark by the end of that year.

02:06:37.140 --> 02:06:40.556
And then in terms of unit utilization, really, really great improvement

02:06:40.588 --> 02:06:45.436
in there, you'll Note that in 2023 our unit utilization was 48.8%

02:06:45.588 --> 02:06:49.100
and we've been able to drop that by 8.4% year on year, which is really

02:06:49.140 --> 02:06:53.340
significant. And I can tell you that our paramedics are seeing that they

02:06:53.380 --> 02:06:57.228
are not as overwhelmed in a 12 hour shift as they were.

02:06:57.364 --> 02:07:00.876
We are seeing a reduction in missed meal breaks for paramedics, for example,

02:07:00.908 --> 02:07:04.172
and a reduction in forced end of shift overtime, which are

02:07:04.196 --> 02:07:07.080
really important for our staff. Next slide please.

02:07:09.140 --> 02:07:12.920
In terms of the 80th percentile response time, there is an improvement of

02:07:13.220 --> 02:07:17.260
about 22 seconds for the whole region between 2023 to

02:07:17.300 --> 02:07:21.564
2024. And we've seen improved response times in all municipalities

02:07:21.612 --> 02:07:24.822
except for North Dumfries. But there's a slight increase in

02:07:24.846 --> 02:07:28.806
North Dumfries of about 28 seconds at the 80th percentile.

02:07:28.918 --> 02:07:32.406
There's a lot of different factors for that. And for a brief

02:07:32.438 --> 02:07:35.590
explanation for you, there are much less calls in North

02:07:35.630 --> 02:07:39.462
Dumfries. So because of that, when there are outlier calls that

02:07:39.566 --> 02:07:44.006
exceed our response time target, they actually affect the average much more significantly

02:07:44.118 --> 02:07:47.814
than they would in some of our urban centers, just because there's less calls to

02:07:47.822 --> 02:07:52.060
compare it against. And the other important thing here is that,

02:07:52.600 --> 02:07:56.256
you know, these response times and the stuff about unit utilization

02:07:56.368 --> 02:07:59.620
does really show that our performance is improving.

02:08:00.200 --> 02:08:03.424
I'm always mindful, Councillor Shantz asked this a number, a

02:08:03.432 --> 02:08:06.832
couple years ago, When will we start to see that the investments are

02:08:06.856 --> 02:08:09.472
working? When will we start to see system improvement?

02:08:09.616 --> 02:08:13.232
And you know, I'm standing in front of you today saying we're starting to see

02:08:13.256 --> 02:08:16.526
that needle really turn and to thank council for their investment

02:08:16.598 --> 02:08:20.110
in us in the last couple of years. The other thing that is

02:08:20.150 --> 02:08:23.630
happening is, as you you are aware, we use a master plan to

02:08:23.670 --> 02:08:27.530
identify our strategic priorities and our investments required over time.

02:08:27.830 --> 02:08:31.850
And so we have a current master plan that technically expires the end of 2027.

02:08:32.150 --> 02:08:35.758
But because of the change in things since the pandemic and

02:08:35.814 --> 02:08:38.766
how much so many things in the system has changed,

02:08:38.878 --> 02:08:42.302
we are hoping to bring forth you a brand new master plan that will take

02:08:42.326 --> 02:08:44.400
effect starting in 2026,

02:08:45.100 --> 02:08:48.164
sometime hopefully May or June.

02:08:48.332 --> 02:08:51.428
And that's being done by a third party consultant who we're working

02:08:51.484 --> 02:08:54.740
really closely with and that will kind of

02:08:54.780 --> 02:08:58.916
set the course for paramedic services from 2026 to 2035.

02:08:59.068 --> 02:09:02.884
We've picked that 10 year timeframe on purpose because that's also when the new hospital

02:09:02.972 --> 02:09:06.580
comes online and changes to the hospital system occurs. So we wanted to

02:09:06.620 --> 02:09:09.814
make sure we captured it in the 10 year window. Next slide

02:09:09.862 --> 02:09:13.190
please. As I mentioned earlier, we're really,

02:09:13.230 --> 02:09:16.742
really pleased about these metrics and they speak for themselves on the

02:09:16.766 --> 02:09:20.646
slide. We were losing an average

02:09:20.678 --> 02:09:23.942
of 32 hours per day in 2024, but that was 42

02:09:23.966 --> 02:09:27.878
hours per day in 2023 for ambulance offload time.

02:09:28.014 --> 02:09:31.094
So that 10 hours per day goes right back into the system,

02:09:31.182 --> 02:09:34.646
which is why response Times are improving, which is why end of shift

02:09:34.678 --> 02:09:38.432
overtime is reducing. It's why all those things are happening. And so

02:09:38.456 --> 02:09:42.176
that's a significant improvement. And kudos to my leadership

02:09:42.208 --> 02:09:45.904
team who meet every two weeks with every single hospital leadership,

02:09:45.952 --> 02:09:49.648
executive leadership to work on this. And I'd

02:09:49.664 --> 02:09:52.960
be remiss if I didn't shout out to the senior executive leadership at all three

02:09:53.000 --> 02:09:56.912
hospitals, they have made this a priority for their entire hospital

02:09:57.016 --> 02:10:00.416
system. Ambulance offload times are now a metric that is shared

02:10:00.448 --> 02:10:04.192
through their entire decision support for, through their whole hospitals,

02:10:04.336 --> 02:10:07.904
which previously ambulance offload time was considered a

02:10:07.912 --> 02:10:11.440
metric only in the emergency department. And so now it's a metric for all three

02:10:11.480 --> 02:10:14.976
hospitals. And we have seen real significant buy in.

02:10:15.128 --> 02:10:18.560
And as you'll see there in Q1, which was January to March,

02:10:18.680 --> 02:10:22.096
at the 90th percentile, it was taking us 124 minutes

02:10:22.128 --> 02:10:25.328
to offload patients, which means 90% of the time we would show up,

02:10:25.384 --> 02:10:29.374
we would be on offload delay for 124 minutes or less. In the

02:10:29.462 --> 02:10:32.622
last quarter of 2024, that dropped to

02:10:32.646 --> 02:10:36.990
50 minutes. And that's really, really significant. That 74 minute improvement

02:10:37.150 --> 02:10:40.846
on 53,000 calls. You can imagine the system improvement

02:10:40.878 --> 02:10:44.414
that creates. So kudos to our hospitals and to my leadership team for

02:10:44.502 --> 02:10:48.250
not giving up on this one. And it's really improved. Next slide please.

02:10:49.750 --> 02:10:53.358
In terms of Code Zero critical capacity, that is formerly

02:10:53.374 --> 02:10:56.654
what was Code Zero, was formerly Code Red, and Code Critical was formerly

02:10:56.702 --> 02:11:00.270
Code Yellow. So you're going to, This will probably be the last time I

02:11:00.310 --> 02:11:03.150
refer to them as Code Red and Code Yellow because we have to get used

02:11:03.190 --> 02:11:06.942
to the new terms. But you'll see that in

02:11:06.966 --> 02:11:10.238
terms of the statistics and it's also in the actual eScribe report,

02:11:10.374 --> 02:11:13.598
you'll see that we've seen real improvement in all areas, which again

02:11:13.654 --> 02:11:17.134
is an indicator of two things. One, council investment in

02:11:17.142 --> 02:11:21.158
the enhancements that we've received and two, the offload delay metrics really also

02:11:21.214 --> 02:11:24.934
do move that needle as well because if we have our resources stuck in

02:11:24.942 --> 02:11:28.290
the hospital, then we're going to have less resources available in the community.

02:11:28.750 --> 02:11:32.070
So we're really pleased about that. And for Code Critical, we don't have as much

02:11:32.110 --> 02:11:35.254
data to report at this point because it is a different threshold

02:11:35.302 --> 02:11:38.502
Now, as I mentioned. previously, Code Yellow was three ambulances available or

02:11:38.526 --> 02:11:42.150
less. Now Code Critical is five ambulances available or less.

02:11:42.270 --> 02:11:45.766
So we would expect the number of Code Critical events

02:11:45.798 --> 02:11:49.474
to increase, but that's okay because they're increasing at a higher threshold,

02:11:49.522 --> 02:11:53.090
which allows us to Put in mitigation strategies to make sure we never get

02:11:53.130 --> 02:11:57.042
down to that Code Zero point of view. So that, So we were expecting that

02:11:57.066 --> 02:12:01.058
bump and we'll give you more analysis on that next year. So that's

02:12:01.074 --> 02:12:03.790
the key performance indicators. Next slide please.

02:12:04.410 --> 02:12:07.890
In terms of our legislative requirement to the province, I do have to report on

02:12:07.930 --> 02:12:11.138
our response time performance plan. And once council accepts this

02:12:11.194 --> 02:12:14.028
report for information, I can submit it to the province by the end of March

02:12:14.084 --> 02:12:18.092
as is required by the legislation. We meet all the Ministry of

02:12:18.116 --> 02:12:21.372
Health compliance targets and we saw a

02:12:21.396 --> 02:12:25.068
decrease in the targets or an improvement in the targets

02:12:25.084 --> 02:12:28.764
for CTAS 1 to 5 responses with a slight increase in sudden

02:12:28.812 --> 02:12:32.348
cardiac arrest responses. And despite all the system

02:12:32.404 --> 02:12:35.836
pressures, we think we're doing really, really well. I would like to explain

02:12:35.868 --> 02:12:39.382
to Council how the RTPP works because it is a kind

02:12:39.406 --> 02:12:42.490
of somewhat convoluted reporting metric.

02:12:43.070 --> 02:12:46.726
So the CTAS levels, which stands for Canadian Triage and Acuity

02:12:46.758 --> 02:12:50.294
Scale, is applied by the paramedic once they arrive scene

02:12:50.382 --> 02:12:53.702
and assess the patient. CTAS 1 is your sickest patient,

02:12:53.766 --> 02:12:57.110
CTAS 5 is your patient who is not as sick

02:12:57.190 --> 02:13:00.902
and everything in between. The challenge with that is

02:13:01.006 --> 02:13:03.930
our response times are measured against that assessment.

02:13:04.270 --> 02:13:07.766
Yet we are not dispatched knowing how sick the patient

02:13:07.798 --> 02:13:11.642
is on the other end, if that makes any sense. So when the

02:13:11.666 --> 02:13:15.130
dispatchers send us on a code four, which is our current thing,

02:13:15.170 --> 02:13:18.858
lights and sirens, we try and get to every single call or they send

02:13:18.874 --> 02:13:22.250
us on a code three, which is a non lights and sirens response. It is

02:13:22.290 --> 02:13:25.802
quite possible that at the end of that code three call, which is not

02:13:25.826 --> 02:13:29.562
urgent, we end up with a patient who's actually a CTAS 1 or

02:13:29.586 --> 02:13:33.242
a sudden cardiac arrest. And we're measured against an

02:13:33.266 --> 02:13:36.590
assessment score we apply upon arrival. So it's a really

02:13:36.630 --> 02:13:39.934
convoluted way, Myself and other chiefs across the province are constantly

02:13:39.982 --> 02:13:43.390
working with the province on how to improve this reporting metric.

02:13:43.550 --> 02:13:46.542
And I do think the biggest improvement will come at the end of the year,

02:13:46.566 --> 02:13:50.078
which I know Council's happy about, which is that's when the new dispatch system

02:13:50.134 --> 02:13:53.870
gets put into Cambridge CACC called Medical Priority Dispatching Service and

02:13:53.910 --> 02:13:57.182
that MPDS is much more in line with CTAS. So we

02:13:57.206 --> 02:14:00.014
do expect that for 2026 anyways,

02:14:00.142 --> 02:14:03.450
we will be able to report in early 2027 that those

02:14:03.490 --> 02:14:07.386
numbers more aligned and hopefully my explanation helped

02:14:07.418 --> 02:14:10.986
with that. Next slide please. As I mentioned,

02:14:11.018 --> 02:14:15.002
we did see a slight decrease or worsening of the response

02:14:15.066 --> 02:14:18.794
times to sudden cardiac arrests. However, the Ministry  of Health sets a target

02:14:18.882 --> 02:14:22.202
of 50% of the time we need to arrive on scene, or a

02:14:22.226 --> 02:14:25.306
defibrillator needs to arrive on scene within six minutes or less.

02:14:25.458 --> 02:14:28.634
This metric is also informed

02:14:28.682 --> 02:14:32.030
by fire department statistics. All of your fire departments provide

02:14:32.070 --> 02:14:35.730
us how quickly they get to sudden cardiac arrest calls with the defibrillator.

02:14:36.070 --> 02:14:39.850
And so we're still well above the ministry target.

02:14:40.310 --> 02:14:43.822
For years, the target for the region of Waterloo has been 70%,

02:14:44.006 --> 02:14:47.838
which gives us something to strive for in terms of best practice and

02:14:47.894 --> 02:14:51.294
system improvement. So you'll probably not hear a recommendation

02:14:51.342 --> 02:14:53.470
from me to lower the target. We want to keep it where it is,

02:14:53.510 --> 02:14:56.686
and we'll just strive to keep it, trying to receive it, knowing we're

02:14:56.718 --> 02:15:00.606
always going to meet the ministry's target or beat it. Next slide,

02:15:00.638 --> 02:15:04.878
please. For the rest of the CTAS levels, you'll see

02:15:04.934 --> 02:15:08.830
in that chart, which is also outlined in the report, an improvement across

02:15:08.870 --> 02:15:12.478
all CTAS times, which is really good. But again,

02:15:12.614 --> 02:15:16.190
I remind you that the CTAS are applied by the paramedics once they get

02:15:16.230 --> 02:15:19.614
there. And so, you know, you could, and I'll give

02:15:19.622 --> 02:15:23.024
you an example, you could get a call for a patient who's a experiencing

02:15:23.072 --> 02:15:25.552
nausea and that's all you think it is. And you arrive there and it's someone

02:15:25.576 --> 02:15:29.056
having a heart attack and suddenly their CTAS jumps. But we're measured

02:15:29.088 --> 02:15:31.968
against the response time, as if we knew it was a heart attack to begin

02:15:31.984 --> 02:15:35.280
with, if that makes any sense. So that's an issue

02:15:35.320 --> 02:15:38.660
that we're continuing to work on provincially and locally.

02:15:39.240 --> 02:15:41.740
A quick reminder that we, Next slide, please.

02:15:43.080 --> 02:15:46.864
A quick reminder that we have changed the response time performance plan targets

02:15:46.912 --> 02:15:50.874
for this calendar year. That was something you approved in the fall. So you'll

02:15:50.922 --> 02:15:55.162
note that all of the targets

02:15:55.306 --> 02:16:00.154
in the second column there for CTAS

02:16:00.202 --> 02:16:04.170
3 through 5 have all increased. And we've done that quite on purpose because

02:16:04.210 --> 02:16:07.322
our system should be designed to prioritize our

02:16:07.346 --> 02:16:11.066
resources for the sickest patients first, just like an emergency department

02:16:11.138 --> 02:16:14.666
would triage the sickest patient first and support that patient first.

02:16:14.818 --> 02:16:18.770
And so this, these new targets

02:16:19.350 --> 02:16:23.214
really do, It kind of says to us, this is what's important to us.

02:16:23.382 --> 02:16:27.214
And it also is in keeping with our comparators across the province. If you look

02:16:27.222 --> 02:16:29.982
at Hamilton, they do the same thing. You look at Halton region, they do the

02:16:30.006 --> 02:16:32.654
same thing. So we wanted to make sure we were comparing apples to apples in

02:16:32.662 --> 02:16:36.414
terms of system performance. Next slide,

02:16:36.462 --> 02:16:39.502
please. In terms of mitigation strategies,

02:16:39.566 --> 02:16:42.654
as I've already mentioned, we're preparing for implementation of the MPDS

02:16:42.702 --> 02:16:45.965
system at Cambridge CACC which will occur on December 10,

02:16:46.106 --> 02:16:49.460
2025. We're actively engaged with the Ministry even though Right,

02:16:49.500 --> 02:16:52.372
The last few weeks they've been in caretaker periods, so we haven't heard much from

02:16:52.396 --> 02:16:56.148
them. But once that gets changes, we'll, we'll continue

02:16:56.204 --> 02:17:00.036
that work. We are not going to take our foot off the gas regarding

02:17:00.068 --> 02:17:03.092
offload delays because we know that there is still more work that our hospitals can

02:17:03.116 --> 02:17:06.628
do, even though they've had amazing improvement and we know that they're committed

02:17:06.644 --> 02:17:10.904
to that work as well. We did just submit a funding

02:17:10.952 --> 02:17:14.536
proposal for, to extend for another year for the designated

02:17:14.568 --> 02:17:18.088
offload nurse program, which has been really, really effective.

02:17:18.264 --> 02:17:21.416
And I can tell you that the Ministry of Health uses the

02:17:21.488 --> 02:17:25.192
DONP program, the example from our

02:17:25.216 --> 02:17:28.792
hospitals here in the region, as a way for other hospitals to say this

02:17:28.816 --> 02:17:31.528
is, this is how we want you to use this money. So we're really pleased

02:17:31.544 --> 02:17:35.128
with that. We want to continue expansion of patient care

02:17:35.184 --> 02:17:38.720
model standards, which are new standards in the legislation that allows us to do things

02:17:38.760 --> 02:17:42.384
like treat and release from the scene, treat and refer, and alternative

02:17:42.432 --> 02:17:45.632
destinations. And if I could, if I could connect that back to the

02:17:45.656 --> 02:17:48.912
HART hub presentation, that the HART hub work and the work

02:17:48.936 --> 02:17:53.104
that those teams do becomes really important to us because we want to establish

02:17:53.152 --> 02:17:57.024
an alternate destination pathway with the HART hub, but also in conjunction with

02:17:57.032 --> 02:18:00.992
that a treat and refer pathway, which means we go to a 911 call,

02:18:01.176 --> 02:18:04.656
Once we know that the patient is eligible for say Sanguen's 

02:18:04.688 --> 02:18:08.535
mobile team to come and take over care, then we'll do a warm handoff

02:18:08.567 --> 02:18:11.815
of care and do and allow that, which means our paramedics

02:18:11.847 --> 02:18:15.127
can be released. I want to make it clear to Council that's a future state.

02:18:15.183 --> 02:18:18.375
We can't do that now because we do need Ministry of Health Emergency Health Services

02:18:18.447 --> 02:18:21.991
branch approval for that. But that is our end state and we think

02:18:22.015 --> 02:18:25.975
that the HART hub is a viable option for that and we're really supportive

02:18:26.007 --> 02:18:30.071
of that model. The other thing as mentioned,

02:18:30.215 --> 02:18:33.744
right now, Council's approved our shift to the high growth scenario for the

02:18:33.752 --> 02:18:36.912
master plan. I do hope that we'll be able to give you

02:18:36.936 --> 02:18:41.328
a master plan in the coming months that allows you to see a sustainable

02:18:41.504 --> 02:18:45.540
investment in paramedic services as populations

02:18:45.880 --> 02:18:49.580
continue to increase as well as call volumes continue to increase.

02:18:50.040 --> 02:18:53.568
And we're going to manage call demand effectively. And then

02:18:53.704 --> 02:18:57.552
finally, the last thing which you'll see in a

02:18:57.576 --> 02:19:01.012
couple of months you'll see report for consent on, is our

02:19:01.036 --> 02:19:04.500
community paramedicine program. And that is work that is

02:19:04.540 --> 02:19:08.180
100% funded. There is no tax levy impact to that. It's 100%

02:19:08.220 --> 02:19:11.508
funded by both Ontario Health and then primarily the Ministry of Long Term

02:19:11.564 --> 02:19:14.996
Care. And we think that the role of community paramedics can play

02:19:15.068 --> 02:19:19.204
in reducing the demand on 911 systems is really significant.

02:19:19.292 --> 02:19:22.884
We're going to continue that work. So that's my report

02:19:22.972 --> 02:19:25.240
and I'm happy to take any questions.

02:19:27.580 --> 02:19:29.960
Thank you. We have a number of questions.

02:19:30.940 --> 02:19:34.388
Excuse me, Councillor Huinink. Thank you,

02:19:34.444 --> 02:19:37.988
Chair. Thank you Chief Riches for coming. Thank you for all this great

02:19:38.044 --> 02:19:41.940
news. It's good to know that our KPIs are improving in

02:19:41.980 --> 02:19:45.760
every sense and that our hospital partners are on board

02:19:46.220 --> 02:19:49.828
and most importantly that your staff and your teams

02:19:49.924 --> 02:19:53.282
are feeling the good and positive differences.

02:19:53.426 --> 02:19:57.394
You alluded to this in noting that we were exemplary

02:19:57.522 --> 02:20:01.218
to the province in terms of the DONP program. But I wonder

02:20:01.234 --> 02:20:05.314
if you want to speak to where this puts our region

02:20:05.482 --> 02:20:08.850
relative to other comparator municipalities.

02:20:08.930 --> 02:20:12.786
Like are we industry leading at this point, are there

02:20:12.938 --> 02:20:16.580
further things that we need to improve upon?

02:20:16.730 --> 02:20:20.112
What's the, how do we compare

02:20:20.176 --> 02:20:23.712
I think is my question, but also just thank you for

02:20:23.736 --> 02:20:27.820
coming. Thank you through you Mr. Chair, Appreciate the question.

02:20:28.600 --> 02:20:32.464
Specifically related to the designated offload nurse program and offload delay

02:20:32.512 --> 02:20:36.016
metrics, Every month the province sends us the previous month's

02:20:36.048 --> 02:20:39.440
metrics for the entire province. And a couple of years

02:20:39.480 --> 02:20:42.620
ago I would argue that the regional Waterloo hospitals were

02:20:42.660 --> 02:20:46.124
probably not in the higher bracket compared

02:20:46.172 --> 02:20:50.028
to other municipalities in the province And locally.

02:20:50.204 --> 02:20:54.092
we are starting to see significant improvement in that regard.

02:20:54.236 --> 02:20:57.772
So last year for example, Guelph General was doing very well, continues to do

02:20:57.796 --> 02:21:01.292
very well with ambulance offload times. But now region of Waterloo hospitals are very

02:21:01.316 --> 02:21:04.684
comparable to Guelph General. I can probably prepare

02:21:04.732 --> 02:21:08.108
something in an email with Commissioner Wang's

02:21:08.124 --> 02:21:11.766
approval to give you a bit more of a fulsome

02:21:11.798 --> 02:21:14.966
response to that on the data in terms of how we to compare to other hospitals,

02:21:14.998 --> 02:21:18.530
if you'd like. Sure. Okay. Thank you. Yep. Thank you.

02:21:19.630 --> 02:21:22.774
Councillor Foxton. Thank you. And Chief,

02:21:22.862 --> 02:21:26.486
what a well done job. I had the privilege of sitting in the last

02:21:26.558 --> 02:21:30.262
master plan and evolving that. And year after year we've come here

02:21:30.286 --> 02:21:34.486
and we've talked about how hard it is and the offloads

02:21:34.518 --> 02:21:37.550
and everything else. And well done, Chief.

02:21:37.850 --> 02:21:41.138
So well done that I don't even mind the 20 seconds delay in North

02:21:41.234 --> 02:21:44.226
Dumfries. Thank you.

02:21:44.298 --> 02:21:48.162
Thank you. Councillor James. Thank you, through you Chair, thank you so

02:21:48.186 --> 02:21:51.922
much for the update and presentation. I do have a question. I'm going to try

02:21:51.946 --> 02:21:55.330
and ask this in the most palatable way,

02:21:55.370 --> 02:21:58.994
I guess. when you're collecting, you mentioned the CTAS and

02:21:59.002 --> 02:22:02.396
the reporting and the discrepancy there. Do you keep the

02:22:02.498 --> 02:22:06.376
data? Is that tracked and how much, Say it's

02:22:06.408 --> 02:22:09.864
a five and it should be a one or vice versa. Is that tracked at

02:22:09.872 --> 02:22:13.820
all and compared, be it locally or provincially?

02:22:14.160 --> 02:22:18.056
And then the second part of that question is, does that impact

02:22:18.208 --> 02:22:23.640
future kind of training for the

02:22:23.680 --> 02:22:27.256
dispatch in terms of recognizing, does it trigger anything

02:22:27.288 --> 02:22:30.990
like that? through you, Mr. Chair, thank you for your question.

02:22:32.090 --> 02:22:35.298
In terms of tracking, the challenge is that the current

02:22:35.354 --> 02:22:39.282
system we use, which council's heard myself and previous chiefs report on,

02:22:39.386 --> 02:22:43.510
is kind of antiquated. It's called DPCI, Dispatch Priority Card Index.

02:22:44.010 --> 02:22:47.550
And it's not a very,

02:22:47.850 --> 02:22:51.390
what's the word I'm looking for, Nuanced or very sophisticated system.

02:22:52.010 --> 02:22:55.356
People say, yes, I'm sweaty, Suddenly that becomes

02:22:55.388 --> 02:22:59.772
a code four, quite honestly. And the

02:22:59.796 --> 02:23:03.628
challenge with that is that when you compare that to paramedic

02:23:03.644 --> 02:23:06.412
applied CTAS, it's hard to compare.

02:23:06.476 --> 02:23:09.644
We do track and when we see things like, hey,

02:23:09.692 --> 02:23:13.276
you gave us this call to us on a non emergency basis, but it turned

02:23:13.308 --> 02:23:17.020
out to be really critical. Yes. There's a conversation between us and the Cambridge

02:23:17.100 --> 02:23:20.398
Central Communication Center leadership. They want to see significant

02:23:20.524 --> 02:23:23.930
improvement as well. I know we work very closely with them on this

02:23:23.970 --> 02:23:27.674
MPDS implementation. They're really, really excited about it.

02:23:27.842 --> 02:23:30.522
I can tell you, Someone else asked me this as well and it's related to

02:23:30.546 --> 02:23:34.266
your question, Councillor James. The ambulance

02:23:34.298 --> 02:23:38.202
communications officers at the Cambridge CACC are going through extensive training

02:23:38.266 --> 02:23:41.434
to get ready for MPDS. It's like 64 hours worth of training

02:23:41.482 --> 02:23:45.082
that they've got to do, which is really significant, a huge investment by the province

02:23:45.146 --> 02:23:49.036
in their staff in that regard. And that also includes them

02:23:49.188 --> 02:23:52.444
becoming accredited under the International Academy of Emergency

02:23:52.492 --> 02:23:56.204
Dispatchers, which means now they have an accreditation standard they have to meet. And we

02:23:56.212 --> 02:23:59.868
all know how important kind of setting gold standards is. So I do

02:23:59.924 --> 02:24:03.132
expect that the new dispatch system will be much, in

02:24:03.156 --> 02:24:06.876
terms of data and data analysis, will be much more easier

02:24:06.908 --> 02:24:10.764
for all of us to manage. And you know, I'd be

02:24:10.772 --> 02:24:14.620
remiss if I didn't compliment the province on its overall strategy to implement

02:24:14.780 --> 02:24:18.428
the MPDS across the entire province. They've now done it, I think in

02:24:18.484 --> 02:24:21.900
eight or nine different CACC centers and they're doing two or more

02:24:21.940 --> 02:24:25.004
before the summer and then you'll see Hamilton,

02:24:25.052 --> 02:24:28.668
CACC and then us. So this is a real big project for the ministry

02:24:28.844 --> 02:24:32.732
and we do expect that we'll be able to

02:24:32.756 --> 02:24:36.172
compare apples to apples a bit more if I can use that analogy for you.

02:24:36.276 --> 02:24:40.310
It is a real challenge though because I

02:24:40.350 --> 02:24:43.798
can say from a staff level it's really frustrating for our staff to think they're

02:24:43.814 --> 02:24:47.958
going to an emergency, rush to the emergency, it turns out not to be one

02:24:48.094 --> 02:24:51.606
and vice versa. It's a real challenge for them to respond

02:24:51.638 --> 02:24:54.822
to a call that they don't think is an emergency and then it turns out

02:24:54.846 --> 02:24:58.086
to be someone really sick on the other end, because how you prepare

02:24:58.118 --> 02:25:01.238
your brain to respond to those calls is differently when you're responding.

02:25:01.334 --> 02:25:04.410
So I think we're all really excited about this improvement.

02:25:05.230 --> 02:25:09.116
I'll share this with. I started in this job in 1995-96.

02:25:09.238 --> 02:25:13.180
and I was told then that MPDS would hit the province by the year 2000.

02:25:13.880 --> 02:25:18.032
So here we are in 2025 and we're getting it. So this

02:25:18.056 --> 02:25:21.424
is long overdue, If I may. Better late than never.

02:25:21.512 --> 02:25:24.848
Thank you so much, Chief. Patience is a virtue.

02:25:24.944 --> 02:25:27.540
Yes. Councillor Shantz.

02:25:29.160 --> 02:25:32.816
thank you. Just want to say I was very excited

02:25:32.928 --> 02:25:36.644
to see the offload delay numbers and

02:25:36.792 --> 02:25:40.476
you mentioned that I spoke about, I've spoken about it a number of times.

02:25:40.548 --> 02:25:44.780
So I'm glad to see that that's improving. And thanks

02:25:44.860 --> 02:25:47.660
to everyone you know,

02:25:47.700 --> 02:25:51.676
involved, the paramedics and

02:25:51.748 --> 02:25:55.000
you too for your leadership. Good job.

02:25:55.780 --> 02:25:59.452
Thank you. If I may, Mr. Chair, it's important and

02:25:59.476 --> 02:26:03.400
I know council recognizes this, This is not a one person

02:26:04.340 --> 02:26:08.316
effort or initiative. We have a team of almost 400 at paramedic

02:26:08.348 --> 02:26:11.996
services and then you think about all the hospitals and their executives and their teams

02:26:12.028 --> 02:26:15.532
and their nurses and their orderlies. Everybody's part

02:26:15.556 --> 02:26:18.940
of the solution. And that's probably the thing if I may Councillor Shantz,

02:26:19.020 --> 02:26:22.412
that we're most excited about is everybody seems to be on board and there's buy

02:26:22.436 --> 02:26:25.932
in across all levels of all the system which really does

02:26:25.956 --> 02:26:29.548
result in system change and that's the improvement we've seen. But I appreciate your comments

02:26:29.604 --> 02:26:32.602
and all the comments of council. I'll make sure I take them back to our

02:26:32.626 --> 02:26:36.138
staff. Thank you. Councillor

02:26:36.154 --> 02:26:38.470
McCabe. Thank you.

02:26:39.170 --> 02:26:42.630
Yeah, and thank you. As others have said for, It's always great,

02:26:42.930 --> 02:26:46.954
as we've seen in some other divisions today, It's always great to see improvements and

02:26:47.042 --> 02:26:50.282
meeting goals and exceeding them. I'm just curious, I wanted to

02:26:50.306 --> 02:26:55.322
make sure I understood what you said about the, the response times. Did

02:26:55.346 --> 02:26:59.402
you say there's 74,000 responses

02:26:59.466 --> 02:27:03.514
like calls that paramedics go to, and 53,000

02:27:03.602 --> 02:27:06.442
that fire and police are also at. no.

02:27:06.466 --> 02:27:09.898
Okay. I'm really actually curious

02:27:09.994 --> 02:27:13.546
how many times do paramedics go when really only paramedics

02:27:13.578 --> 02:27:17.018
are needed, but you can give me different numbers, whatever numbers you've

02:27:17.034 --> 02:27:20.314
got in your head. Okay. So through you, Mr. Chair, if I can

02:27:20.322 --> 02:27:24.504
clarify that, and I appreciate your question. So we,

02:27:24.672 --> 02:27:27.880
because of workload, one of the metrics we look at is

02:27:27.920 --> 02:27:31.208
not just the number of calls that we're required to respond to, but how many

02:27:31.264 --> 02:27:34.060
resources we ascend to those calls.

02:27:34.720 --> 02:27:38.136
So in 2024, we did just

02:27:38.208 --> 02:27:41.592
under 54,000 unique 911 calls that

02:27:41.616 --> 02:27:45.288
paramedic services had to respond to. Within those

02:27:45.344 --> 02:27:48.744
54,000, because of the complexity of the call or the

02:27:48.752 --> 02:27:52.794
different reasons, we needed certain resources, there were 74,000

02:27:52.882 --> 02:27:56.470
vehicle responses to those calls. So that's the connection there.

02:27:58.050 --> 02:28:01.450
So hopefully that cleared that up. I don't have the data in front of me

02:28:01.490 --> 02:28:05.226
today to tell you how many of those calls police and fire also responded

02:28:05.258 --> 02:28:08.938
to. I can tell you that we work closely with the fire departments

02:28:08.954 --> 02:28:12.378
on tiered response agreements as well with police on what

02:28:12.434 --> 02:28:16.502
calls can we, do we both need to go to. Do you

02:28:16.526 --> 02:28:20.086
go to versus we go to. And that's

02:28:20.118 --> 02:28:23.142
an ongoing moving target because as you can appreciate,

02:28:23.286 --> 02:28:27.110
all three emergency services have different legislative mandates. So sometimes we all

02:28:27.150 --> 02:28:30.854
have to go, even though, like, just legislation requires us all to

02:28:30.862 --> 02:28:34.102
go, Even though maybe only police are needed or

02:28:34.126 --> 02:28:38.422
only us are needed. Right. And so when the, when the medical priority

02:28:38.486 --> 02:28:42.790
dispatch or new, like the new system's in place, will that help

02:28:42.830 --> 02:28:45.894
with some of that? Or, I mean, I guess, like, will it do, you know,

02:28:45.902 --> 02:28:49.590
if there's legislative or regulatory changes that are going to accompany that.

02:28:49.630 --> 02:28:53.478
So it's like the ones who really need to be there only are

02:28:53.614 --> 02:28:57.334
having to respond. through you, Mr. Chair, great question, Councillor McCabe.

02:28:57.382 --> 02:29:00.950
We, we have not received necessary a

02:29:00.990 --> 02:29:05.334
legislative mandate to make changes. However, we are actively

02:29:05.382 --> 02:29:08.250
working with all the local fire departments about Right

02:29:08.290 --> 02:29:11.270
Sizing the tiered response agreements that we have.

02:29:12.210 --> 02:29:16.106
And we're, I've assigned a superintendent directly

02:29:16.138 --> 02:29:19.898
to the MPDS implementation, and part of his job is

02:29:19.954 --> 02:29:23.466
to kind of, let's, let's make sure all the tiered response agreements

02:29:23.498 --> 02:29:27.146
work for everybody. We have almost the same tiered response agreement

02:29:27.178 --> 02:29:30.666
for the, currently for the three urban municipalities because

02:29:30.818 --> 02:29:34.298
they're all very similar in times in terms of call volumes and type,

02:29:34.314 --> 02:29:38.138
volume call types and those types of things. We have different tiered

02:29:38.154 --> 02:29:42.106
response agreements for the four townships,

02:29:42.298 --> 02:29:45.770
and we want to make sure, And we have some commitment from local area

02:29:45.810 --> 02:29:49.162
Fire chiefs to discuss how do we make sure that the

02:29:49.186 --> 02:29:53.110
right calls, the fire department is responding to the right medical assist calls and

02:29:54.370 --> 02:29:58.522
we just right size it all. When I speak to the fire chiefs,

02:29:58.666 --> 02:30:02.268
all of them are quite aware on the demand on their resources for medical

02:30:02.324 --> 02:30:05.692
responses and they want to make sure that everything is right size so

02:30:05.716 --> 02:30:09.212
that nobody wants, none of us, none of the emergency services want to go to

02:30:09.236 --> 02:30:12.924
a call that they were not needed at. Like let's just make that clear because

02:30:13.012 --> 02:30:16.268
it's, it's a demand on our resources, on our staffing, it costs

02:30:16.284 --> 02:30:20.492
money, like all those types of things. So I

02:30:20.516 --> 02:30:24.236
don't know when we will change the tiered response criteria. But one of the lessons

02:30:24.268 --> 02:30:28.464
we've learned for other municipalities who implemented MPDS before ours

02:30:28.592 --> 02:30:32.320
was have these discussions early and often before the light

02:30:32.360 --> 02:30:36.340
switch gets flipped and we've done that. So we are hoping to produce

02:30:38.600 --> 02:30:42.512
more current and more future ready tiered response agreements with our

02:30:42.536 --> 02:30:46.144
fire departments. That's really good to hear. And just a quick,

02:30:46.192 --> 02:30:49.360
if you could, can you give an update on where we're at with the alternative

02:30:49.440 --> 02:30:52.784
destination site clinics?

02:30:52.832 --> 02:30:56.698
I can, Yeah, through you Mr. Chair, unfortunately we've

02:30:56.714 --> 02:31:00.442
not seen the progress on that project as much

02:31:00.466 --> 02:31:03.834
as we would like. And that's why we're as excited as we

02:31:03.842 --> 02:31:08.270
are about the HART hub because right now the model,

02:31:09.010 --> 02:31:12.490
we don't have a viable model right now to do an alternative destination

02:31:12.570 --> 02:31:16.310
too. But we really believe that the HART Hub model could provide us that.

02:31:17.090 --> 02:31:20.314
And so we're really trying to

02:31:20.482 --> 02:31:23.240
support the HARTHub model because that's where the funding is.

02:31:23.280 --> 02:31:27.016
And at this point, unless I am mistaken,

02:31:27.208 --> 02:31:30.600
the funding for the ICC model has not come through. I'm looking

02:31:30.640 --> 02:31:32.980
at Dr. Wang and she's nodding her head like this.

02:31:34.160 --> 02:31:37.368
So unfortunately that project is, I would say

02:31:37.424 --> 02:31:40.120
behind where we want it to be in terms of outcome at this point.

02:31:40.240 --> 02:31:43.592
But we're looking at any and

02:31:43.616 --> 02:31:46.260
all alternatives that we can to Change it.

02:31:48.970 --> 02:31:51.310
Councillor Liggett. 

02:31:52.490 --> 02:31:54.190
Thank you, Mr. Chair. John,

02:31:55.530 --> 02:31:58.190
do we have ambulances on order now?

02:31:59.610 --> 02:32:03.490
Through you Mr. Chair, yes, we, we put an order in in the summer of

02:32:03.530 --> 02:32:07.458
2024 to support our 2025 and 2026

02:32:07.514 --> 02:32:10.642
enhancements and replacements. And where are we

02:32:10.666 --> 02:32:13.314
at with that? Are they, do you think they're going to get, do they have

02:32:13.322 --> 02:32:17.144
to go back and forth like cars or are they a one shot

02:32:17.192 --> 02:32:20.456
deal? Where are they coming, I'm assuming they're coming from the States. Maybe I shouldn't

02:32:20.488 --> 02:32:23.736
assume that. Maybe we're good at manufacturing ambulances.

02:32:23.848 --> 02:32:27.176
so can we talk to that, especially with all the announcements today.

02:32:27.328 --> 02:32:31.260
So through you, Mr. Chair, and a great question, Councillor Liggett.

02:32:32.960 --> 02:32:36.340
The ambulances, the patient care compartments are built in Canada,

02:32:36.640 --> 02:32:39.800
but the chassis that they're, That they go on is

02:32:39.840 --> 02:32:42.518
from the U.S. just like many, many other vehicles.

02:32:42.704 --> 02:32:46.106
And so, you know,

02:32:46.178 --> 02:32:49.514
I don't have a final number for you yet. We are working with the vendor,

02:32:49.562 --> 02:32:52.602
with the purchase order to understand if there's going

02:32:52.626 --> 02:32:55.786
to be a change in the chassis price as a result of what you're speaking

02:32:55.818 --> 02:32:59.546
about, Councillor Liggett. And it's

02:32:59.578 --> 02:33:02.922
not just ambulances for paramedic services. There is other equipment that.

02:33:02.946 --> 02:33:06.442
Unfortunately, some of the equipment does come through the U.S. Even

02:33:06.466 --> 02:33:09.858
though we're buying from a Canadian company who then Canadianizes it.

02:33:09.914 --> 02:33:13.618
So we are working on the impact that that will have

02:33:13.674 --> 02:33:17.218
on us from a capital planning perspective and working with Finance and

02:33:17.354 --> 02:33:21.470
Commissioner Wang and to try to understand what that impact looks like.

02:33:24.090 --> 02:33:27.906
Okay, thank you, Chief Riches.

02:33:28.018 --> 02:33:31.778
These reports were both for information, so no action is

02:33:31.834 --> 02:33:35.212
required from committee. Thank you. Thank you very much. Thanks for

02:33:35.236 --> 02:33:37.080
the, And the good news as well.

02:33:39.140 --> 02:33:43.084
9.1. Council tracking. There are no items on the tracking

02:33:43.132 --> 02:33:45.640
list. Is there any other business?

02:33:48.820 --> 02:33:52.014
Okay, our next meeting, April 8,

02:33:52.237 --> 02:33:55.372
2025. Is there someone prepared to

02:33:55.396 --> 02:33:58.604
move that we adjourn this meeting? Councillor McCabe.

02:33:58.652 --> 02:34:01.826
Seconded by Councillor Wolf. Use your eScribe buttons

02:34:01.858 --> 02:34:03.150
to indicate your support.

02:34:22.730 --> 02:34:25.604
That's carried. Thank you.
